Bartholin Cyst
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Unilateral posterior introital mass, Pain with walking/sitting, Fluctuance on palpation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-005
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, throbbing vulvar pain preventing the patient from sitting or walking, requiring immediate assessment for an abscess.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated rapid onset of severe, localized vulvar swelling and throbbing pain causing inability to mobilize or sit. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to differentiate between an uncomplicated cyst and an acute Bartholin abscess requiring immediate drainage or aggressive antibiotic therapy to prevent systemic spread prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: This lump appeared a few days ago, but today it blew up, and the throbbing pain is so intense I cannot even sit down or walk to the bathroom.
History of Present Illness: The patient noticed a small, painless lump in the labia majora three days ago. Over the past 12 hours, she developed severe, localized, throbbing vulvar pain. She reports extreme discomfort and difficulty with walking, sitting, and wearing tight clothing. She notes a unilateral swelling at the posterior introital margin. She denies spontaneous rupture or purulent discharge at this stage. She reports feeling generally unwell with mild chills but hasn't measured her temperature.
Functional Status: Functionally impaired; confined to lying on her side due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Difficulties with activities of daily living due to severe pain on mobilization.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient is lying on her side, visibly wincing when attempting to move, and appearing distressed by the localized pain.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary inspection reveals a 4cm unilateral swelling at the posterior introital margin (left 4 o'clock position). The area exhibits marked erythema, induration, and significant warmth over the cyst. Palpation, which is exquisitely tender, demonstrates clear fluctuance indicating a confined fluid or pus collection. No spontaneous drainage observed. Inguinal lymph nodes are mildly enlarged and tender on the left side.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment, roommate available to assist with immediate needs.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol 1g every 6 hours with minimal relief. No other regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Bartholin Gland Abscess - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Sebaceous Cyst - Unlikely due to the specific location at the posterior introitus and the rapid, severely painful progression to an abscess.

Syphilitic Chancre - Ruled out as chancres are typically painless, ulcerated lesions, unlike this exquisitely tender, fluctuant, intact mass.

Vulvar Hematoma - Unlikely given the absence of antecedent trauma and the presence of localized heat, erythema, and low-grade fever suggesting infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to impaired mobility)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, functionally limiting vulvar pain and a low-grade fever. Objective clinical examination confirmed a fluctuant, erythematous, and exquisitely tender unilateral mass at the posterior introitus, diagnostic of an acute Bartholin abscess. The severity of the pain, resulting in an inability to sit or walk, combined with early systemic signs of infection (tachycardia and low-grade fever), necessitated urgent after-hours assessment. Delayed care until the in-hours period would have risked spontaneous, uncontrolled rupture, worsening systemic infection, and prolonged unmanageable pain, firmly meeting the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ceftriaxone 1g and prescribed oral cephalexin 500mg four times daily alongside metronidazole 400mg twice daily to cover typical pathogens. Provided strong oral analgesia (ibuprofen 400mg + paracetamol 1g). Arranged for an urgent in-hours GP or gynecological clinic appointment tomorrow morning for definitive incision and drainage (Word catheter placement).
Positive Behavioral Change: To encourage spontaneous drainage and provide pain relief, perform warm sitz baths for 15 minutes three times a day; experiencing the soothing effect of the warm water will reinforce the habit of localized self-care. To prevent worsening infection, take the prescribed antibiotics exactly on time; understanding that this halts the spread of bacteria will ensure strict adherence.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Sitz bath basin recommended.
Follow-up: Urgent review tomorrow morning for definitive surgical management (incision and drainage).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if you develop a high fever (over 38.5 C), if the redness rapidly spreads outward into the groin or thigh, or if the pain becomes completely unmanageable despite the new medications.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The current treatment is a temporizing measure; surgical drainage is almost always required for definitive resolution.
Clinical Course: The antibiotics and pain relief will prevent the infection from spreading and take the edge off the pain, but the swelling will likely remain until it is formally drained or ruptures on its own.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you get a high fever, severe chills, or notice the redness spreading rapidly.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The gland that lubricates the vaginal opening has become blocked and infected, forming an abscess. The antibiotics will stop the infection from spreading to your bloodstream, but the pus needs to be physically drained by a doctor tomorrow for you to feel completely better.
Actionable Steps: Start the antibiotics immediately, take regular pain relief, use warm sitz baths to encourage the abscess to point, and attend the urgent appointment tomorrow morning.

